.TH PCBVEC 1
.SH NAME
pcbvec \- write the layers of a board as vectors
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pcbvec
[
.B \-L
.I lib
] ...
.B \-o
.I dir
.I board.pcb
.RI < board.lock
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Pcbvec
writes into
.I dir
a
.IR vec (5)
file for each layer
.IR pcbgerber (1)
writes a Gerber for, of the same name (top.vec, in1.vec, ...,
topsilk.vec, topcourt.vec), and drill.vec, the holes as disks.
Both draw the layers by one description, so the vectors are the
Gerbers.
Each file begins with its layer record, KiCad's name of the layer
(F.Cu, In1.Cu, ..., B.Cu, F.Mask, F.Paste, F.Silkscreen, F.Courtyard,
B.Silkscreen, B.Courtyard, Edge.Cuts, Drill), so a viewer knows it by
what it is, not by its name.
The records of a part are bracketed by its name, and those of a net by
its; a plane is a zone of its net; each part's box is named, for
.IR pcbview (1).
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
pcbvec -o view ch1.pcb < ch1.lock
cd view && pcbview bottom.vec in1.vec top.vec topsilk.vec edge.vec drill.vec
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
pcb(5), vec(5), pcbgerber(1), pcbview(1)
